<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Armenian Oblast</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1828">1828</a>–<a href="/years/1840">1840</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Armenian Oblast was a province of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> formed in 1828 from the Erivan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nakhchivan">Nakhichevan</a> khanates ceded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-turkmenchay">Treaty of Turkmenchay</a>. Many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> from Iran resettled there. It was abolished in 1840 and its lands were later reorganised as the Erivan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/governorate">Governorate</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/armenian-oblast.jpg" alt="Armenian Oblast" width="320" height="435" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Brindz · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_arms_of_Armyanskaya_Oblast.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Armenian Oblast was a province of the Caucasus Viceroyalty, with its centre at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yerevan">Yerevan</a>, called Erivan in Russian. Its lands correspond to most of present-day central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, the Iğdır Province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nakhchivan">Nakhchivan</a> exclave of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>. It was created after the Russo-Iranian War of 1826–1828, encouraged by Russian officials who wished to reward <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> for supporting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and who believed Christian Armenians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgians">Georgians</a> would back Russian rule over the many Muslims of the Caucasus. Ivan Paskevich, the commander in the war, was made Count of Erivan in the year of its creation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>According to the historian George Bournoutian, the Russians kept the former Iranian administrative structure in place. Few Russian officials served there, and they depended on Muslim administrators and interpreters of the old regime; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/land-tenure">land tenure</a>, taxes and the courts remained virtually unchanged, and Persian or the local Turkish dialect stayed in use. Bordering a defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, the province was largely ignored by the viceroyalty in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tiflis</a> and by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a>.</p><p>Russian surveys of 1829–1832 counted 99,411 inhabitants on the eve of the conquest, 75 per cent Muslim and 25 per cent Armenian. Article XV of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-turkmenchay">Treaty of Turkmenchay</a> let Iranian subjects of the Azerbaijan Province migrate freely, and from 1828 to 1831 some 35,560 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>; another 21,666 came from the Ottoman pashaliks of Bayazid and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kars">Kars</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-turkish-war-1828-1829">Russo-Turkish War</a> of 1828–29. By 1832 the population was 164,450, about half Armenian and half Muslim.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1829 the explorer Friedrich Parrot of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-tartu">University of Dorpat</a>, with the Armenian writer Khachatur Abovian, made the first recorded ascent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-ararat">Mount Ararat</a> from the monastery of St. Hakob in Akhuri. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> and Russian officials complained of the high-handed Muslim administrators, an 1840 decree set aside local laws and customs, required business in Russian and Russian staff in every office, and merged the oblast into the new Georgian-Imeretian Province. From 1844 the area was part of the Tiflis Governorate, until the Erivan Governorate was formed in 1849.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Armenian Oblast” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Combined with Nakhichevan, it became the <b>Armenian Oblast</b>, and many Armenians from Iran settled there.</blockquote><a href="/state/erivan-khanate">Erivan Khanate</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Russo-Persian War of 1826-1828, Ehsan Khan Kangarlu went over to Russia, and the Treaty of Turkmenchay in 1828 ceded the khanate to the Russian Empire, which joined it with Erivan in the <b>Armenian Oblast</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/nakhichevan-khanate">Nakhichevan Khanate</a></li></ul></section>
